SM(489,{"n":"1 John 1.1-10","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 John 1.1-10.mp3","d":2661.7,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.24,"Okay, so we'll go ahead and get started."],[22.4,"And when we get started, we're starting the first epistle of St."],[27.68,"John. So I'm gonna do a little introduction."],[31.28,"As anybody who has paged ahead has probably noticed or might just know in general."],[41.76,"So I'm just gonna sort of do an intro for all three of the Johannine epistles."],[48.56,"Um, and then we'll sort of move on from there."],[51.04,"And when we get to the end of 1 John, we'll just go straight into probably a quick succession."],[57.68,"Second and second and third John."],[62.12,"So in terms of uh introducing 1 John, 1 John is relatively unique."],[71.72,"And in most of these uh intros, I've had to go into all kinds of this and that about dating and who wrote it."],[82.2,"Not because there was a lot of doubt with a lot of the other epistles and stuff, but just because there's a lot of modern scholars who like to muddy the waters to make them seem deep."],[91.44,"Um, and so kind of clarifying that, 1 John's kind of remarkable in that there's not a ton of dissent."],[101.42,"Like even 19th century Germans. were like, this is written by the same person who wrote St"],[108.02,"John's Gospel. Like it's when you read it in in Greek, it's sort of painfully obvious that they were written by the same person."],[116.71,"And I say painfully obvious because as I probably mentioned in uh the introduction to the Gospel of John five years ago now, uh that um the Greek's kind of horrible uh by Greek standards."],[133.44,"By the standard is of like native Greek speakers in Greece."],[137.68,"Um that's masked a lot from uh people who are primary English speakers, people who are first language English."],[148.16,"So if you study Biblical Greek from English, and I don't know how true this is in other languages, I imagine it's true probably in most Germanic languages."],[160.84,"I don't know how true it is in Romance languages, uh, and it may be completely untrue in like Semitic and Asian languages, but um with the English language, first John is usually the first book of the Bible you read, followed by selections from St."],[178.52,"John's Gospel. Uh, and the reason for that is that from the perspective of someone whose first language is English, it's really easy."],[189.72,"Because St. John's Greek is basically in both the Gospel and the Epistle is basically like subject, verb, object."],[201.86,"Like it looks very much like an English sentence."],[205.75,"And so that's how a normal English sentence is structured."],[209.13,"You have the subject, you have the verb, you have, you know, maybe an indirect object, then the direct object."],[215.26,"And so we think that's good."],[216.78,"And so when you're first learning Greek at that early stage, you're you're basically just translating each word."],[225.23,"And so if you're at that phase and you take a sentence from Saint John and you translate each word, you get a nice English sentence at the end."],[234.67,"So that makes it relatively easy to read."],[237.03,"So that's why First John is done first."],[239.99,"Um and then selections from from Saint John's Gospel."],[245.03,"But uh the way English sentences are structured is not how Greek sentences were typically structured in the ancient Greek language by people who are native Greek speakers."],[258.39,"So I have literally been in a graduate school classical Greek class, and had the professor as a prank pass out pages from Saint John's Gospel in order to freak out the students."],[277.41,"That's how different it is than classical Greek."],[279.73,"Like they didn't know what it was, they didn't know what to do with it"],[285.69,"So, from the perspective of a very educated Greek person, the Greek is horrible."],[291.71,"From our perspective, as native English speakers, those of us who are native English speakers, it's sort of delightfully simple."],[301.15,"So it's very clear, concise Greek, you know."],[304.99,"Um but so because of that, because it's in such an unusual weird style by Greek standards, that makes it really clear that these are coming from the same person."],[317.87,"Because you wouldn't expect two different people to produce something that odd, you know, in style."],[326.37,"Like, you know, when you're picking up a James Joyce book."],[330.99,"Like, there's not anyone else who writes that way, unless they're deliberately trying to write like James Joyce."],[336.4,"So, um, that's always been a sort of a lock from the original readers."],[343.52,"Um, all of the the the church fathers talk about St."],[349.6,"John's gospel and his epistle, as connected."],[353.94,"So the only people who um really have tried to cast doubt on that were certain folks in the in the 20th century who really needed to write dissertations and publish journal articles."],[368.06,"And their attempts to try to act like they were written by different people are kind of desperate and sad."],[374.78,"I'm being really blunt tonight, but oh well."],[380.06,"Because I mean they really make arguments like, well, as we'll see when we're reading 1 John, all the time in 1 John, he uses these uh conditional constructions."],[392.22,"If this, then that."],[394.22,"Whereas if this, then that."],[396.6,"Does that all the time."],[397.8,"And you don't see those in St."],[399.16,"John's Gospel. Now that might at first cause you to think, well, oh, well, that is interesting."],[406.06,"That seems like a different writing style."],[407.98,"Until you think, this is an epistle."],[410.86,"St. John's gospel is narrative."],[414.02,"How would you use if then statements in a narrative?"],[418.42,"And then after that, if Jesus went to Jerusalem, then"],[423.23,"Of course he didn't use that construction."],[425.71,"He's writing narrative."],[426.76,"But it's that level of stuff."],[429.74,"It's, well, okay, let's ignore like all of church history and let's ignore all these things."],[434.78,"And let's just take these two texts like they dropped out of the sky."],[439.18,"And see if we can find differences in the grammar and stuff."],[442.54,"And we'll also ignore that they're completely different genres."],[446.06,"That's sort of the only way anybody can do that."],[450.78,"And they didn't get a lot of traction."],[452.78,"There were some significant. known named scholars in the 20th century who had a go at that"],[460.5,"But pretty much everyone agrees that they're written by the same person."],[466.9,"Also, pretty much everyone agrees they're written somewhere near the end of the first century AD."],[476.18,"Or the very first couple of years of the second century, like 101, 102 at the latest."],[481.06,"So everyone agrees on that too."],[484.82,"So then the interesting part, this is gonna be more me bashing modern scholars again, is that uh uniformly, if you pick up the scholarly books, they'll say, Well, they were written by the same author, but they weren't written by St."],[499.06,"weren't written by St. They"],[499.78,"John the son of Zebedee."],[499.98,"John the Apostle."],[505.52,"And you might wonder, well, why would they say that?"],[509.2,"And your guess is as good as mine, because they don't have an argument."],[515.12,"And if you think about it, there isn't really a possible argument."],[519.58,"Because if St."],[520.62,"John the Apostle, uh they definitely don't think he wrote Revelation."],[523.6,"So if St."],[524.16,"John the Apostle didn't write the epistle and he didn't write the gospel, we don't have anything he wrote."],[532.56,"So if we don't have anything he wrote, we can't compare what he wrote to these texts to find out if he wrote them."],[540.21,"So you can't use that as an argument."],[542.61,"Usually when someone argues for pseudopigraphy, meaning someone argues that this text is claiming to be written by someone who didn't write it, they argue that, well, they're trying to claim the authority of that person."],[560.11,"So you'd think, well, maybe they could, maybe they could make an argument that, oh, these texts were written by somebody else, but they're trying to claim the authority of St."],[568.99,"John the Apostle."],[571.39,"Problem with that argument would be if these aren't written by St."],[575.39,"John the Apostle, what is St."],[576.83,"John the Apostle famous for?"],[583.72,"So what authority would they be claiming if he didn't write these?"],[589.86,"Because he's like a minor character in Matthew, Mark, and Luke."],[593.78,"He doesn't do much in Matthew, Mark, and Luke."],[595.78,"He's a central character in St."],[597.86,"John's Gospel only if he's the beloved disciple."],[603.03,"But the author claims to be the beloved disciple."],[606.23,"So if he's not the author, that's not him."],[611.59,"So trying to claim his authority doesn't make any sense, because I mean that would be like saying he's trying to claim the authority of St."],[617.83,"Simon the Zealot, one of the other apostles."],[620.32,"Like. Okay. We don't have a lot of info."],[627.97,"So there really is no like positive constructible argument that this wasn't written by Saint John the Apostle, Saint John the son of Zebedee."],[639.77,"And over against their sort of just general suspicion, that any biblical book was written by the person it's named after, other than just having that as a presupposition."],[650.48,"Um you have literally all of church history."],[657.52,"And when I say all of church history, we're going back to like Saint Irenaeus and Saint Justin Martyr and Saint Polycarp."],[665.44,"Saint Polycarp literally knew Saint John the Apostle and said he wrote thee."],[674.2,"Saint Irenaeus and Saint Justin Martyr, both disciples who learned from Saint Polycarp, they both said Saint John the Apostle wrote these."],[687.72,"They literally went to theological school, these second century saints in Ephesus, where Saint John the Apostle taught who was the author of these books."],[699.48,"So that general suspicion also requires that all these early church fathers of the second century were just lying for some reason."],[711.24,"Either they didn't know the author."],[714.57,"But it can't be they didn't know the author, because even the people who say it wasn't St."],[718.69,"John the Apostle say it was written at the end of the first century in Ephesus by the same person."],[726.37,"So whoever wrote it has to be the person they knew."],[729.89,"So then you'd have to believe that they were lying that the person they knew was St."],[734.37,"John the Apostle, when it was really some other John."],[738.21,"And that again raises the question: well, why would they do that?"],[741.09,"Because if he didn't write these things, St."],[742.85,"John the Apostle is famous for nothing."],[747.85,"So, all that is to say."],[750.47,"Um, barring people just wanting to dissent for the sake of dissent, the fact that St."],[758.63,"John the Apostle wrote St."],[760.07,"John's Gospel and 1 John is as close to a lock as you could get."],[766.72,"Um, with even most like generally skeptical scholars ultimately, basically, at least accepting that it's probable."],[774.58,"There are a lot of them who won't commit, but they're like, yeah, probably."],[778.04,"Um, because they know they don't have a good argument otherwise."],[781.56,"Uh, when we get to the introduction to the book of Revelation, that'll be a totally different case."],[787.64,"Um, and that gets a lot more complicated."],[790.36,"Uh, but for for 1 John and St."],[793.88,"John's Gospel, it's pretty clear-cut."],[797.0,"Now, you may have noticed I keep saying 1 John and not mentioning 2nd and 3 John."],[803.08,"And the reason. for that is that most of the fathers don't mention 2nd and 3rd John"],[810.32,"And we're pretty much not sure why."],[815.68,"Because early scripture collections that have 1 John also have 2nd and 3 John."],[825.6,"Just nobody talks about them."],[827.52,"So it's likely that it's because they're so small, because they're like a paragraph, that they were sort of treated as addenda to 1 John."],[838.64,"And that kind of thing is not uncommon."],[842.48,"So, for example, in the Old Testament, if you're using Hebrew Bible or your typical uh Protestant Bible, you've got Jeremiah and Lamentations."],[856.84,"If you are uh using a uh Catholic Bible or the Orthodox Study Bible, you've also got the book of Baruch in there and the Epistle of Jeremiah."],[870.28,"Those up to four books all get quoted by the uh church fathers as just being Jeremiah."],[881.88,"And uh even today in the Orthodox Church, we just did this in Holy Week."],[887.44,"Uh, we also do it at one point uh on Christmas Eve, actually, we read from the book of Baruch, but it'sing introduced as the prophecy is from Jeremiah when we're do the dialogue."],[900.52,"Because what's called the Book of Baruch and Lamentations and uh the Epistle of Jeremiah are all sort of come from Jeremiah."],[910.44,"Like there's the book of Jeremiah, and then there's sort of this other Jeremiah stuff."],[914.54,"The Epistle of Jeremiah is really short."],[916.24,"It's just sort of all lumped into Jeremiah when the when the fathers talk about it."],[922.77,"Um so I think it's very possible that that is the that is what's going on."],[930.93,"Because we don't see them saying, we talked about when we talked about 1st and 2nd Peter, that there are fathers. who you see distinguishing between the two"],[941.21,"There were church fathers who would say, we only accept the first epistle of St."],[945.45,"Peter. Or who would say, we accept both epistles of St."],[948.57,"Peter. We've received both."],[950.44,"But you don't find that with St."],[952.2,"John. They talk about St."],[953.64,"John's epistle, but you don't see him saying, we only received the first epistle of St."],[958.04,"John and not second and third."],[960.62,"They're so short that I don't know what you'd take offense at."],[963.2,"Like, no, this is heresy."],[966.3,"We don't see that kind of negotiation happening."],[969.82,"But they tend to just refer to the epistle of St."],[973.02,"John. And then when we find the actual text, second and third John are stuck in there."],[978.14,"And you don't find a lot because they're so short. you don't find a lot of people of Fathers quoting 2nd or 3 John for us to even be able to verify certain things"],[993.46,"Well, they are three epistles."],[995.62,"They are three epistles."],[997.86,"But there are times where they just treat really small books by the same author as if they're like appendices to the main one."],[1007.24,"And talk about it as if it's just one thing with a couple attachments."],[1011.09,"Like there's also this is subtly different, but we also find a lot of like little bits and liturgical elements in biblical texts. that have this sort of weird s weird status"],[1028.78,"So if you came on one of the Monday nights during uh Great Lent in Great Compline, we read the prayer of Manasseh, King of Israel."],[1038.22,"The prayer of Manasseh is found in pretty much all of the Greek editions of the Old Testament that we have."],[1045.58,"But you don't find it listed on the table of contents."],[1048.54,"It's like a paragraph."],[1050.4,"It's just sort of in there."],[1051.84,"So Psalm 151."],[1055.44,"And it's usually not like it's not like they tacked it onto the end of the book of Psalms."],[1060.64,"It's sort of in like an appendix tacked onto the like in an appendix or something."],[1064.88,"Like it was uh some kind of hymn or something."],[1067.2,"if Now you figure you're paying to have a book copied."],[1072.22,"You're putting out this big expenditure."],[1074.4,"You might as well, you know, throw in a couple extra pages."],[1078.01,"And put some of that stuff in it that you're going to use, like when you're in church or when you're worshiping at home or that kind of thing."],[1083.93,"So it's also possible 2nd and 3rd John, because they're so short so short, were sort of like that."],[1088.25,"They just, anytime somebody was copying 1 John, they just kind of got lumped in because there was room, you know, and you're you're you're going to the expense."],[1098.01,"So 2nd and 3rd John seem to be largely non-controversial because they're always included, but they're not non-controversial in the sense that we see all these church fathers saying, yes, 2 andnd 3rd John, these are important canonical books."],[1111.29,"They just don't talk about them much at all"],[1116.05,"And they just sort of show up right in all the Bibles."],[1123.65,"Yeah, so uh since I teased this before we started recording, um I mentioned that pretty much everybody agrees that this was written to the the church at Ephesus uh at the at the end of the first century."],[1140.45,"I said almost everyone, not talking about those modern people primarily, but talking about this weird thing with St."],[1147.01,"Augustine. Saint Augustine did a commentary on 1 John, and he labels it, he titles it Saint John's Epistle to the Parthians."],[1166.91,"Which would be like Persians, essentially."],[1173.71,"And nobody knows why."],[1177.23,"So if you do need to write a dissertation or a journal article, there you go."],[1183.63,"But he's the first instance."],[1186.11,"Like there's nobody before that who calls it."],[1188.83,"But that sort of takes root in the Latin tradition."],[1194.93,"And we're not sure if it takes root in the Latin tradition because it was already there in the Latin tradition, and St."],[1200.33,"Augustine is just referring to it, or if later people were like referring back to St."],[1205.13,"Augustine. Because you get to like the Venerable Bede over in England, and he talks about John the Apostle writing an epistle to the Parthians."],[1215.77,"And we're like."],[1230.85,"There was. I mean, there is."],[1235.89,"I mean, maybe there are places where uh in this epistle St."],[1241.25,"John will uses examples of like light and darkness and stuff."],[1245.73,"So maybe St."],[1248.37,"Augustine or somebody thought he's writing against Zoroastrians or manich or something."],[1257.25,"But it's a stretch."],[1258.9,"I'm scratching to try to make that make sense."],[1260.98,"So nobody's sure where St."],[1262.42,"Augustine got that."],[1263.46,"I mean, I wouldn't guess he made it up."],[1265.86,"He's getting it from somebody or someplace."],[1269.3,"Um but yeah, that is an odd thing."],[1273.7,"So just to throw that in."],[1278.18,"And um, there is one."],[1283.62,"I'm gonna get this wrong, probably."],[1285.46,"I'm probably gonna remember this wrong."],[1286.66,"I think it's Codex Vaticanus."],[1288.62,"One of the great like fourth and fifth century biblical texts, like manuscripts of the whole Bible, um has 2 John labeled as being to the Parthians."],[1302.94,"Sort of rarely."],[1303.82,"But so there's your Parthians for the day."],[1306.54,"Um nobody's sure sort of where that came from."],[1309.82,"But uh as near as I could tell, it that's at least unhelpful."],[1315.5,"Because to try and make that make sense that it's to the Parthians, you have to so like stretch the stuff of the epistle that um rather than that helping you interpret it, that just causes you to have a weird mangled interpretation."],[1331.1,"And um, with all respect to St."],[1336.5,"Augustine, especially since we discovered the Dead Sea Scrolls in the middle part of the 20th century, um, pretty much everyone now has come around to thinking that uh both St."],[1350.26,"John's Gospel and this epistle are incredibly steeped in the Judaism of the first century."],[1359.86,"So there was a period where uh there are a lot of in the epistle we're gonna see, in addition to those like light and darkness motifs, he'll talk about children of the light and children of the darkness."],[1373.64,"Children of God, children of the devil, these sort of dualisms."],[1376.76,"So there was a period there in the early the early 20th century, uh, especially guys like Rudolf Boltmann, where they got kind of excited about this whole dualism thing, and they're like, Gnosticism, it's Gnostic."],[1390.25,"Gnosticism, dualism, ah."],[1392.89,"Um But after the finding of the Dead Sea Scrolls, like there's literally one of the Dead Sea Scrolls is titled The War of the Children of Light Against the Children of Darkness."],[1403.85,"And these were and the community at Qumran who wrote the Dead Sea Scrolls were literally like Jewish fundamentalists."],[1410.81,"These were people who thought the Pharisees had gone liberal."],[1414.01,"Literally. Literally. That's why they went out in the desert to start their own community."],[1419.35,"So they were not Gnostic or they were no Greek ideas floating around out there."],[1423.59,"They were as Jewish as you get"],[1427.63,"And you find these same kind of ideas and motifs."],[1434.43,"So all that Bing Long introduction done."],[1438.51,"Unless anybody has any questions or comments."],[1445.47,"To the Christian community in Ephesus."],[1449.63,"Yes. Yeah. Yeah."],[1453.07,"And we're gonna we're gonna see."],[1455.47,"I don't want to get into too much of this now, but when we get to the book of Revelation, we're gonna see Ephesus and its environs are what Revelation is written to, also."],[1465.71,"Um, Ephesus, we talked about this a little in the introduction to Ephesians, but Ephesus was an incredibly major port in the Roman Empire at this time."],[1474.71,"Um, and so it was a major hub."],[1478.15,"It was actually um sorry to our Roman Catholic friends, but uh it was a much more important city to early Christianity than Rome was."],[1493.75,"But I mean, Ephesus really was the hub early on."],[1497.21,"We see it as a hub in the book of Acts for St."],[1500.09,"Paul's missionary journeys, taking off and coming back to Ephesus."],[1505.37,"St. Saint John is there, St."],[1507.21,"Mary Magdalene is there, Mattheotokos is there at different points."],[1513.54,"It is the major hub, from which these different missionary journeys are going out, where they're returning to."],[1520.68,"When Saint Paul knows he's going probably to his death, he goes and says goodbye to the presbyters at the church in Ephesus."],[1529.88,"So it's a major, major hub."],[1537.48,"To the point that if you ever get into liturgical studies, all of the liturgies we do in the Orthodox Church are technically liturgies of the Ephesian school."],[1550.76,"They're called that because it develops out of there."],[1553.04,"And as I mentioned already, all of the major church fathers of the second century, like St."],[1558.72,"Justin Martyr, St."],[1560.08,"Irenaeus, St. Polygarp, are all in and around Ephesus and around the apostles there and this community."],[1568.81,"So this is sort of critically important."],[1571.85,"And you know, go we lose sight of it going forward in church history because it basically gets wiped out."],[1577.39,"And so after that it sort of disappears."],[1581.53,"Um, but it was at first uh incredibly important."],[1587.05,"Okay, so unless we have any other questions, comments, insults directed at me."],[1594.01,"Don't insult each other."],[1595.21,"I can take it though."],[1596.81,"Uh we'll go ahead and pick up in uh first epistle of St."],[1600.41,"John, not to the Parthians."],[1600.57,"Chapter 1, verse 1"],[1611.73,"That which was heard from the beginning, which we have heard, which we have seen with our eyes, which we have looked upon, and our hands have handled concerning the word of life."],[1622.93,"We'll go ahead and pause it right there."],[1624.45,"Let's do verse 1."],[1628.21,"So you can already probably see themes from the beginning of St."],[1633.09,"John's Gospel. So remember, as we've pointed out time and time again, and it's important here too, we're used to seeing word."],[1644.27,"Word of God."],[1645.47,"In our modern mind, we think Bible."],[1649.64,"It's not the Bible."],[1652.33,"St. John's Gospel, chapter one, when he says the word became flesh, he's not talking about the Bible."],[1657.43,"The word is Christ."],[1658.73,"So the word of life here is Christ."],[1661.42,"And that's borne out by the fact that the word of life is someone in this case that he heard, he saw."],[1674.71,"He looked upon, handled with his hands."],[1680.84,"So you already have that major theme from the first. chapter of St"],[1685.76,"John's gospel, the idea of the word becoming flesh."],[1688.74,"That the word, the logos as life, has become flash, physical."],[1694.94,"Encounterable by Saint John."],[1696.62,"And notice that it's that which the that which is referring to the word."],[1704.87,"So the word who was from the beginning."],[1707.53,"In the beginning was the word, is how the gospel starts."],[1712.14,"So clearly, thematically, we're drawing on the same ideas here."],[1720.03,"Verse 2. The life was manifested, and we have seen and bear witness and declare to you that eternal life which was with the Father and was manifested to us."],[1735.35,"I don't know why in the Orthodox Study Bible they didn't capitalize the L in life."],[1742.97,"Because clearly that's also talking about Christ."],[1746.61,"The life that was with the Father and was manifested to us is talking about Christ."],[1752.53,"Not just life in some vague sense."],[1756.1,"So again, the word was with God, the word was God."],[1760.6,"He was in the beginning with God."],[1762.2,"Same ideas here."],[1764.06,"He's saying, now this is what we proclaim to you."],[1768.42,"Now, what I'm communicating to you is what I saw and heard when the eternal Christ revealed himself to us."],[1778.82,"Verse three, that which we have seen and heard, we declare to you that you also may have fellowship with us."],[1785.78,"And truly our fellowship is with the Father and with His Son Jesus Christ."],[1803.3,"Making it totally clear."],[1804.9,"So this person, second person of God, the Divine Son"],[1811.72,"This is the person who was manifest to us, revealed to us in the person of Jesus Christ, and who we touched, saw, heard, experienced."],[1822.37,"And we're passing that on to you."],[1825.09,"And the word fellowship here is kind of a Mambi Pambi translation."],[1829.73,"Um, at least in terms of how we use the word fellowship now."],[1833.73,"We use the word fellowship for like, hey man, let's go have a couple cup of coffee and chat, you know, good fellowship."],[1839.78,"Um, that's not what St."],[1841.86,"John is getting at here."],[1843.76,"So the word that's translated fellowship here is uh probably better translated, maybe communion, but it's or participation, even it's the the word that like Plato uses to describe how an object relates to the eternal form."],[1865.02,"It participates in it."],[1866.86,"So if something is beautiful, it's participating in eternal beauty."],[1871.71,"And that's why it is beautiful."],[1874.83,"So that kind of relationship is the relationship he's talking about."],[1879.15,"So he's saying not only did I see Christ, like I watched him walk around and I heard things he said."],[1886.68,"And I even went up and touched him once, but he's saying that he had this kind of communion with Christ and still has it, and that the purpose for him communicating that to them is that that. so that they can have same kind of communion with Christ"],[1905.34,"Which is much closer than just like Jesus was my friend, he can be your friend too."],[1910.96,"This is this is a deep communion."],[1914.66,"Where we become like him that he's talking about."],[1920.1,"Verse 4, and these things we write to you that your joy may be full."],[1925.96,"Maybe filled up."],[1931.72,"Verse 5. This is the message which we have heard from him and declare to you."],[1937.4,"So notice, notice the way this is part of St."],[1942.28,"John's style. I mentioned already that it's odd."],[1948.21,"But his style very much sort of circles back on itself."],[1953.19,"He'll repeat things but change one or two words."],[1957.61,"And so it's written, not it's not when St."],[1961.13,"Paul makes an argument when we went through St."],[1963.45,"Paul's epistles, he sort of argues from point A to point B."],[1967.39,"He'll say, Here's point A that we all agree about."],[1969.49,"Here's Abraham. Here's this."],[1971.5,"And then he'll he'll lay out his argument and get you to point B and say, Therefore, we know this in Christ."],[1978.99,"A to B."],[1980.11,"St. John doesn't work that way."],[1981.47,"It's more like sort of a spiral."],[1983.89,"Where he keeps kind of circling. back"],[1985.93,"He goes like one step forward and then he backs up and then moves a little bit more forward."],[1991.03,"Or like an inchworm."],[1993.67,"Um that cyclical pattern actually exists in in St."],[1999.51,"John's Gospel, too, though it's not as obvious."],[2002.17,"St. John's Gospel is structured around three cycles of the Jewish feasts."],[2007.21,"And so it kind of circles around."],[2009.69,"And when we get into the book of Revelation, we're going to see the same thing."],[2013.37,"But if you've ever read Revelation before, you know that like you keep getting these sevens."],[2018.25,"You get like seven trumpets and then seven bowls, and then seven this and then seven that."],[2022.01,"There's the same kind of cyclical thing that happens, sort of in in terms of his writing."],[2028.65,"But so this epistle already, I think it's pretty clear."],[2029.21,"He's you know, repeat and then add and then repeat and then add"],[2036.77,"So this is the message which we have heard from him and declare to you."],[2040.46,"He's saying, What we heard, we declare to you."],[2042.3,"This is what we heard and what we declare to you."],[2045.5,"That God is light, and in him is no darkness at all."],[2050.62,"Okay. Or your Parthian Zoroastrian thing."],[2061.42,"Um, Zoroastrianism. They believe that there's basically one God of Hura Mazda, but that he has a shadow."],[2069.58,"That sort of he contains also evil right within himself."],[2072.94,"Whereas St John is setting up the exact opposite."],[2081.22,"God is light, him there's no darkness at all."],[2086.26,"Darkness is other."],[2090.34,"Verse 6: if we say that we have fellowship with him and walk in darkness, we lie and do not practice the truth."],[2099.06,"So here's where understanding what is being translated as fellowship here is important."],[2105.32,"So if fellowship is this real intimate communion where you're becoming like someone."],[2110.46,"Where you're sharing their life with them."],[2113.1,"And you say, I'm sharing my life with God, but you're walking in darkness."],[2121.15,"Then we know that your claim isn't true because in God there's only light."],[2126.99,"In his life, there's only light."],[2129.95,"Notice also that in the previous verses, he was talking about fellowship with Christ, and now he's talking about fellowship with God in the same terms."],[2138.43,"So he's pretty clearly indicating that Christ is God."],[2143.79,"That's not some later development after the New Testament."],[2149.31,"Verse 7 But if we walk in the light, as he is in the light, we have fellowship with one another, and the blood of Jesus Christ, his son, cleanses us from all sin."],[2163.27,"So he takes a little swerve there."],[2166.81,"So he said, on the one hand, if we say we're in communion with God, but we walk in darkness, well then we're lying because he's all light."],[2174.41,"So you might expect him to say, but if we walk in the light, then we have communion with God."],[2179.37,"But what he actually says is if we walk in the light, then we have communion with each other."],[2185.77,"We have that relationship with each other."],[2189.61,"And what he's doing there, he's going to develop."],[2192.09,"He, in this epistle, very much like we saw with St."],[2194.57,"Peter and with St."],[2195.45,"Paul, he's laying out the themes here at the beginning that he's going to develop throughout throughout the rest of the epistle."],[2201.93,"So this connection between communion with God and communion with each other is something he's going to be developing out."],[2209.89,"But he sort of drops that in here."],[2211.89,"And then he adds this extra element of within that communion with each other."],[2220.39,"He connects it to that communion we have with each other."],[2223.99,"The blood of Christ, the Son of God, cleanses us from all sin."],[2232.55,"That's important for a couple reasons."],[2234.31,"Some of them are only going to come out later, but right off the bat here, when St."],[2239.43,"John talks about walking in darkness and walking in light, he can't mean by walking in light being perfectly sinless."],[2249.02,"Because if he meant, if we walk in the light, by that he meant you are perfectly sinless and never sin."],[2255.06,"You wouldn't need the blood of Christ to cleanse you of all sin."],[2260.54,"So he's not walking in darkness is not somebody who sins."],[2264.78,"And walking in the light is someone who never sins."],[2268.86,"But that walk has an ongoing nature."],[2273.12,"It's present tense verb."],[2277.12,"So it's not talking about individual actions, it's talking about a way of life."],[2283.76,"But even the person who shares communion with God in Christ and shares communion when we share communion with each other in a church, we still sin."],[2292.42,"But we know that there is forgiveness available."],[2296.94,"Now that forgiveness here is expressed as purification."],[2302.16,"Which is also important."],[2305.92,"And specifically, when we're talking about blood, sacrificial blood purifying things, it's talking about ritual purification."],[2314.64,"That's important because that means the idea of forgiveness that we're gonna get here in St."],[2319.12,"John's Epistle is not based on any kind of legal conception."],[2326.72,"When someone is found guilty of a crime in a court, you don't purify them"],[2334.29,"You don't purify them with blood."],[2335.9,"They get get they punished, or they save the penalty, of that crime and that conviction."],[2341.72,"Or someone is exonerated, or someone has clemency shown to them, or they're pardoned."],[2347.38,"But none of those involve cleansing and washing and purification."],[2353.46,"So sin here is being seen as something that affects the being of the person."],[2360.74,"We're gonna see this play out in the epistle."],[2363.94,"And so forgiveness is then about healing and restoration and transformation."],[2370.0,"The restoration and the building of that communion and that fellowship, not about like getting out of consequences or having someone mad at you not be mad at you anymore."],[2381.58,"And specifically, to be even more specific, there's one particular ritual, and St."],[2391.02,"John's going to come back to this several times in the epistle."],[2395.1,"One particular ritual in the Torah where things are purified with blood."],[2399.74,"That's the Day of Atonement."],[2402.94,"So it's in the Day of Atonement when you have the two goats, the one goat has all the sins put on it."],[2408.78,"That one gets sent out into the desert to Azazel."],[2412.27,"Because the demons are the sort of origin of sin, and so you send it back to them, return to sender."],[2417.73,"Um that goat is not sacrificed because now it's unclean."],[2421.71,"It's got all the sins in it."],[2423.99,"The other goat, the goat for Yahweh, the God of Israel, is sacrificed and its blood is drained, and that blood is taken and used to purify the tabernacle."],[2436.63,"Sort of wash and clean the physical objects in the tabernacle to purify them from sin."],[2444.73,"To restore them to purity and for use."],[2446.73,"And so we'll come back to this, but he's already sort of seeding this in."],[2451.63,"So we hear being purified by the blood of Christ so much in church that the connection to the Day of Atonement, has dropped out."],[2460.42,"But to the original people reading this, that's the ritual from the Old Testament they would be thinking of."],[2467.9,"Because there aren't any other ones really."],[2470.72,"Where that precise kind of thing happens."],[2477.28,"Verse 8, if we say that we have no sin, we deceive ourselves, and the truth is not in us."],[2485.28,"So again, he's hammering on."],[2486.88,"And it's we."],[2490.3,"Not they. So he's talking about the people in the community."],[2495.52,"The people in the Christian community, even the ones who are walking in the light."],[2500.2,"That if they say, so if we say, that we don't have sin, then we we deceive ourselves and the truth is not in us, truth should probably be capitalized there too."],[2513.08,"Because remember St."],[2514.12,"John's gospel, Christ says he is the truth, he is the way, the truth, and the life."],[2520.84,"So the idea is then by saying, if we say that we are without sin, we're lying to ourselves, but that also means that we don't have this communion with Christ."],[2534.84,"Because if we did, that wouldn't make us perfect, but that would make us understand our sin, and that we need this purification by coming to know him."],[2547.4,"Verse 9 If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins. and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness"],[2557.92,"So notice how again, forgiveness and cleansing are paralleled here."],[2564.45,"And what is confession here?"],[2567.89,"Confession is the opposite of saying that you're without sin."],[2575.89,"So confession is very literally talking about verbalizing."],[2582.74,"Standing before God and saying, this right is who I am, this is what I've done, this is the way I've lived my life."],[2592.31,"Rather than hiding it, denying it, rejecting it."],[2595.27,"When we come out and confess it, then we can receive that forgiveness, that purification from it."],[2604.67,"Verse 10, if we say that we have not sinned, we make him a liar, and his word is not in us."],[2610.51,"Again, I think W should be capitalized there because it's not talking about the Bible isn't inside us."],[2617.53,"It's talking about Christ again."],[2620.59,"Why would that make him a liar?"],[2623.71,"Well, if it's Christ's blood that purifies us from sin, and we say we don't need it."],[2630.45,"That shows that we're not in communion in Christ, and we're we're saying that Christ is a liar because Christ felt that he needed to die for our sins."],[2639.25,"Listen. next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the whole counsel of God"],[2646.65,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2652.01,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.24,"lv":2,"h":"Intro to the Johannine epistles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting <b>1 John</b> - one intro for all <b>three Johannine epistles</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Then straight into <b>2 and 3 John</b> in quick succession after 1 John</li><li class=\"d0\">1 John \"relatively unique\": other intros needed lots on dating/authorship because modern scholars \"muddy the waters to make them seem deep\"</li><li class=\"d1\">1 John: not much dissent - even <b>19th-c. Germans</b> said same author as <b>John's Gospel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in Greek \"painfully obvious\" it's the same person</li></ul>"},{"t":116.71,"lv":3,"h":"John's Greek: easy for English readers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Greek is \"kind of horrible\" by native-Greek standards (said in the Gospel intro five years ago)</li><li class=\"d0\">Masked from <b>English first-language</b> readers</li><li class=\"d1\">1 John usually the <b>first book</b> you read in Biblical Greek, then selections from the Gospel</li><li class=\"d1\">John's Greek is <b>subject-verb-object</b> - like an English sentence</li><li class=\"d1\">early on you translate word by word → nice English sentence</li></ul>"},{"t":245.03,"lv":3,"h":"Greek speakers: \"horrible\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not how native Greek sentences were structured</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Grad classical Greek class</b>: professor pranked students with pages of John's Gospel - they \"didn't know what it was\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Educated Greek: horrible; native English speaker: \"delightfully simple\" - clear, concise</li><li class=\"d0\">Odd style = clearly <b>same person</b> for both</li><li class=\"d1\">like a <b>James Joyce</b> book - no one writes that way unless imitating him</li></ul>"},{"t":336.4,"lv":3,"h":"Same author: 20th-c. doubts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Church fathers always link John's Gospel and epistle</li><li class=\"d0\">Doubters: 20th-c. folks needing dissertations / journal articles - \"desperate and sad\" (\"being really blunt tonight\")</li><li class=\"d1\">argument: 1 John constantly uses <b>conditionals</b> (\"if this, then that\") - not in the Gospel</li><li class=\"d1\">but epistle vs. <b>narrative</b> - how use if-then in narrative?</li><li class=\"d1\">ignores church history and that they're <b>different genres</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Little traction; some named scholars tried; nearly all agree same author</li></ul>"},{"t":466.9,"lv":2,"h":"Date and author: John the Apostle","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Near-universal: written near <b>end of 1st c.</b> or first years of 2nd (<b>101-102</b> at latest)</li><li class=\"d0\">Yet scholarly books: same author, but <b>not John son of Zebedee, the Apostle</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"your guess is as good as mine\" - no argument; no possible one</li><li class=\"d0\">They deny he wrote <b>Revelation</b> too → nothing he wrote to compare against</li><li class=\"d0\">Pseudepigraphy usually argued as <b>claiming authority</b> of a name</li></ul>"},{"t":560.11,"lv":3,"h":"What authority would be claimed?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">If not by John the Apostle, what's he famous for?</li><li class=\"d1\">minor in <b>Matthew, Mark, Luke</b></li><li class=\"d1\">central only as the <b>beloved disciple</b> - but the author claims to be that</li><li class=\"d0\">Claiming his authority makes no sense - like claiming <b>Simon the Zealot's</b></li><li class=\"d0\">No positive, constructible argument against John son of Zebedee</li></ul>"},{"t":639.77,"lv":3,"h":"All of church history says John","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Against mere suspicion that any book is by its named author: <b>all of church history</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Polycarp</b> knew John the Apostle and said he wrote these</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Irenaeus</b> and <b>Justin Martyr</b>, disciples of Polycarp, say the same</li><li class=\"d1\">2nd-c. saints \"went to theological school\" in <b>Ephesus</b> where John taught</li><li class=\"d0\">Opponents' view: early fathers all <b>lying</b></li><li class=\"d1\">can't be ignorance - even skeptics say same author, <b>Ephesus, end of 1st c.</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so \"another John\" - why would they lie, if John is famous for nothing?</li><li class=\"d0\">John the Apostle wrote Gospel and 1 John: \"<b>as close to a lock as you could get</b>\"; skeptics mostly say \"probably\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Revelation</b> intro: \"totally different case\", more complicated</li><li class=\"d0\">Fathers: Gospel and epistle connected as one author's work</li></ul>"},{"t":797.0,"lv":2,"h":"Second and Third John","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Most fathers <b>don't mention 2 and 3 John</b> - not sure why</li><li class=\"d0\">Early scripture collections with 1 John include them</li><li class=\"d0\">Likely so small (paragraph each) they were treated as <b>addenda to 1 John</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not sure why - early collections including 1 John also have 2 and 3 John</li><li class=\"d0\">Nobody talks about them, only about \"the epistle of John\"</li></ul>"},{"t":842.48,"lv":3,"h":"Parallel: Jeremiah, Baruch, Lamentations","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew/Protestant Bible: <b>Jeremiah + Lamentations</b>; Catholic / OSB add <b>Baruch</b> and <b>Epistle of Jeremiah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">up to four books quoted by fathers as just \"Jeremiah\"</li><li class=\"d1\">still today: <b>Holy Week</b>, and <b>Christmas Eve</b> reading from Baruch introduced as prophecy of Jeremiah</li><li class=\"d0\">Contrast with Peter: some fathers accepted only <b>1 Peter</b>, some both</li><li class=\"d1\">no such sorting for John - too short to take offense: \"no, this is heresy\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Fathers say \"the epistle of John\"; 2 and 3 John stuck in the text; few quote them</li></ul>"},{"t":1011.09,"lv":3,"h":"Appendices and liturgical bits","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Subtly different: biblical texts with a weird status</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Prayer of Manasseh</b> - read at <b>Great Compline</b> on Lenten Mondays</li><li class=\"d1\">in Greek OT editions but not in the table of contents; a paragraph</li><li class=\"d1\">same with <b>Psalm 151</b> - in an appendix, like a hymn</li><li class=\"d0\">Paying for a copy - \"throw in a couple extra pages\" you'd use in church or at home</li><li class=\"d0\">2 and 3 John probably lumped in with 1 John when copying - <b>room</b> and expense</li><li class=\"d0\">Not controversial because always included, but fathers don't say much</li></ul>"},{"t":1123.65,"lv":2,"h":"Written to Ephesus - or the Parthians?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Nearly everyone: written to the <b>church at Ephesus</b>, end of 1st c.</li><li class=\"d0\">Oddity: <b>Augustine's</b> commentary titled \"<b>St. John's Epistle to the Parthians</b>\" (Persians)</li><li class=\"d1\">nobody knows why; first instance; takes root in <b>Latin tradition</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Bede</b> in England also says epistle to the Parthians</li><li class=\"d1\">maybe light/darkness read as against <b>Zoroastrians / Manichees</b> - \"a stretch\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Augustine got it from someone, \"wouldn't guess he made it up\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Codex Vaticanus</b> (probably) labels <b>2 John</b> \"to the Parthians\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Unhelpful: to make sense of it you stretch the epistle → \"weird mangled interpretation\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1331.1,"lv":3,"h":"Dead Sea Scrolls vs. Gnosticism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">After the <b>Dead Sea Scrolls</b> (mid-20th c.): Gospel and epistle steeped in <b>1st-c. Judaism</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Motifs: <b>light and darkness</b>, children of light / darkness, children of God / of the devil</li><li class=\"d1\">early 20th c., <b>Rudolf Bultmann</b> et al.: dualism → \"<b>Gnostic</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">But a Scroll: <i>War of the Children of Light Against the Children of Darkness</i></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Qumran</b> = \"Jewish fundamentalists\" who thought the Pharisees had gone liberal - left for the desert</li><li class=\"d1\">\"as Jewish as you get\"; no Greek ideas</li></ul>"},{"t":1438.51,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: Ephesus as the hub","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: written to the Christian community in Ephesus? - Yes; Revelation also written to <b>Ephesus and environs</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ephesus: major <b>Roman port</b>, hub - \"more important to early Christianity than <b>Rome</b>\" (apologies to Catholic friends)</li><li class=\"d1\">hub in <b>Acts</b> - Paul's missionary journeys leave from and return to it</li><li class=\"d1\">there at different points: <b>John, Mary Magdalene, the Theotokos</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul, knowing he's headed to death, says goodbye to the <b>presbyters of Ephesus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">All our liturgies technically <b>liturgies of the Ephesian school</b></li><li class=\"d0\">2nd-c. fathers - <b>Justin, Irenaeus, Polycarp</b> - in and around Ephesus with the apostles</li><li class=\"d0\">Lost sight of later: basically \"wiped out\"; at first incredibly important</li></ul>"},{"t":1596.81,"lv":2,"h":"1 John 1:1 - The Word of life","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reading: \"That which was heard from the beginning ... concerning the <b>word of life</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Themes of the <b>start of John's Gospel</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Word\" - we think Bible, but it's <b>Christ</b>: \"the word became flesh\"</li><li class=\"d1\">word of life is <b>someone</b> he heard, saw, looked upon, <b>handled with his hands</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the <i>logos</i> as life became flesh - <b>encounterable</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"That which\" refers to the Word <b>from the beginning</b> = \"In the beginning was the Word\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1720.03,"lv":3,"h":"1:2 - The life manifested","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reading: life manifested, \"we have seen and bear witness ... <b>eternal life which was with the Father</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">OSB doesn't capitalize <b>Life</b> - should: it's <b>Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same as \"the word was with God, the word was God\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Meaning: what I saw and heard when the <b>eternal Christ revealed himself</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Again not \"life\" in some vague sense - the life that was with the Father is <b>Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Declaring what the apostles witnessed first-hand</li></ul>"},{"t":1778.82,"lv":2,"h":"1:3-4 - Fellowship = communion","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reading v.3: \"that you also may have <b>fellowship</b> with us ... with the Father and with His Son Jesus Christ\"</li><li class=\"d0\">The Divine Son, second person, manifest in <b>Jesus Christ</b> - touched, saw, heard; passing it on</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Fellowship\" a \"<b>namby-pamby</b>\" translation - not coffee and chat</li><li class=\"d1\">better <b>communion</b> or <b>participation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Plato's word for how an object relates to the <b>eternal form</b> - beautiful thing participates in eternal beauty</li><li class=\"d0\">He had communion with Christ and still does; purpose is that they have it too</li><li class=\"d1\">\"much closer than Jesus was my friend\" - <b>deep communion</b>, we become like him</li><li class=\"d0\">v.4: \"these things we write that your <b>joy may be full</b>\" - filled up</li></ul>"},{"t":1931.72,"lv":2,"h":"1:5 - The message; John's spiral style","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reading v.5: \"This is the message which we have heard from him and declare to you\"</li><li class=\"d0\">John's style <b>circles back</b> - repeats, changes a word or two</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul argues <b>A to B</b> (Abraham, therefore in Christ); John a <b>spiral</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one step forward, back up, a little further - \"an <b>inchworm</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Also in the Gospel: structured around <b>three cycles of Jewish feasts</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Revelation</b> same: seven trumpets, seven bowls, sevens cycling</li><li class=\"d0\">Here: repeat, then add, repeat, then add</li></ul>"},{"t":2036.77,"lv":3,"h":"1:5 - God is light","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"What we heard, we declare\": <b>God is light, and in him is no darkness at all</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Zoroastrianism (the Parthian idea): one God, <i>Ahura Mazda</i>, with a <b>shadow</b> - evil within himself</li><li class=\"d1\">John sets up the <b>exact opposite</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Darkness is <b>other</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Dualism but not <b>Zoroastrian</b> - evil is not within God</li></ul>"},{"t":2090.34,"lv":2,"h":"1:6 - Claiming communion, walking in darkness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reading: \"if we say we have fellowship with him and <b>walk in darkness</b>, we lie and do not practice the truth\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Communion = intimate, sharing life, becoming like him</li><li class=\"d1\">claim to share God's life while walking in darkness → <b>claim isn't true</b>, in his life only light</li><li class=\"d0\">Earlier fellowship with <b>Christ</b>, now with <b>God</b> in same terms</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ is God - \"not some later development after the New Testament\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2149.31,"lv":2,"h":"1:7 - Walk in the light","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reading: \"if we walk in the light ... we have fellowship <b>with one another</b>, and the <b>blood of Jesus Christ</b> cleanses us from all sin\"</li><li class=\"d0\">A \"swerve\": expect communion with God; he says communion with <b>each other</b></li><li class=\"d1\">lays out <b>themes</b> to develop through the epistle (like Peter, Paul)</li><li class=\"d1\">communion with God ↔ communion with each other</li><li class=\"d0\">Then connects <b>blood cleansing</b> to that communion</li></ul>"},{"t":2232.55,"lv":3,"h":"Not sinlessness: a way of life","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Walking in light can't mean <b>perfectly sinless</b> - else no need for Christ's blood to cleanse</li><li class=\"d0\">Walking in darkness ≠ someone who sins; light ≠ never sins</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Present tense verb</b> - ongoing: a <b>way of life</b>, not individual actions</li><li class=\"d0\">Even in communion with God and in church we still sin - but <b>forgiveness is available</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2296.94,"lv":3,"h":"Forgiveness as purification","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Forgiveness expressed as <b>purification</b> - sacrificial blood = <b>ritual purification</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So not a <b>legal conception</b></li><li class=\"d1\">convicted criminal isn't purified with blood - punished, penalty paid, exonerated, clemency, pardon</li><li class=\"d1\">none involve cleansing or washing</li><li class=\"d0\">Sin affects the <b>being</b> of the person</li><li class=\"d0\">Forgiveness = <b>healing, restoration, transformation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">building communion - not escaping consequences or someone no longer mad at you</li></ul>"},{"t":2381.58,"lv":3,"h":"Day of Atonement","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">One Torah ritual John will return to: the <b>Day of Atonement</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Two goats: one bears all sins, sent to <b>Azazel</b> in the desert</li><li class=\"d1\">demons = origin of sin → \"<b>return to sender</b>\"; not sacrificed - unclean</li><li class=\"d0\">Goat for <b>Yahweh</b> sacrificed; blood drained, used to <b>purify the tabernacle</b></li><li class=\"d1\">wash and clean its objects, restore to purity and use</li><li class=\"d0\">We hear \"purified by the blood of Christ\" so much the <b>Day of Atonement</b> link dropped out</li><li class=\"d0\">Original readers would think of it - no other such ritual</li><li class=\"d0\">Only ritual in the Torah where this precise thing happens</li></ul>"},{"t":2477.28,"lv":2,"h":"1:8 - If we say we have no sin","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reading: \"if we say that we have no sin, we <b>deceive ourselves</b>, and the <b>truth</b> is not in us\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>We</b>\" not \"they\" - people in the community, even those walking in light</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Truth</b> should be capitalized - Christ: \"the way, the truth, and the life\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Saying we're without sin = lying, and no communion with Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">with it we'd not be perfect but would <b>understand our sin</b> and need purification</li></ul>"},{"t":2547.4,"lv":2,"h":"1:9 - Confession","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reading: \"If we confess our sins, he is <b>faithful and just</b> to forgive ... and cleanse us from all unrighteousness\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Forgiveness and cleansing paralleled again</li><li class=\"d0\">Confession = opposite of saying you're without sin</li><li class=\"d1\">literally <b>verbalizing</b>: standing before God - this is who I am, what I've done, how I've lived</li><li class=\"d1\">not hiding, denying, rejecting</li><li class=\"d0\">Confess → receive <b>forgiveness and purification</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not hiding it: then we can <b>receive</b> that forgiveness</li></ul>"},{"t":2604.67,"lv":2,"h":"1:10 - Making him a liar","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reading: \"if we say that we have not sinned, we make him a liar, and his <b>word</b> is not in us\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Word</b> capitalized - Christ, not the Bible</li><li class=\"d0\">Why a liar? It's <b>Christ's blood</b> that purifies - saying we don't need it</li><li class=\"d1\">shows no communion with Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">calls Christ a liar - he felt he needed to <b>die for our sins</b></li></ul>"}]});
